TI  - The Effects of Exercise Intervention on Physical Self-Perceptions and Social Physique Anxiety in China University Girls
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE 21ST PAN-ASIAN CONGRESS OF SPORTS AND PHYSICAL EDUCATION, VOL 4: APPLICATION OF PHYSIOLOGY AND PSYCHOLOGY IN SPORTS
CP  - 21st Pan-Asian Congress of Sports and Physical Education
AB  - The objective of this study was to examine the effects of a 4-month exercise intervention program on physical self-perception (PSP) and social physical anxiety (SPA) of non-physically activity adolescent China university girls An experimental design with randomization into an intervention groups were used The Group A offered to the intervention group twice a week for 4-month, which activity was badminton The Group B offered to the intervention group twice a week for 4-mionth, consisted of badminton sessions followed by 15min of discussions regarding a healthy lifestyle Twenty participants in the Group A and twenty in Group B completed the Physical Self-Perception Profile (PSPP) and the Social Physique Anxiety Scale (SPAS) at pre-test and post-test, in addition, physical fitness, weight and height were measured The intent to treat analysis showed significant improvements in SPA and PSPP sub domains, but Sport Competence (SC) and Physical Strength (PS) were no significant improvement By contract Group A and B, Bodily Attractiveness (BA) and Physical Self-Worth (PSW) were significant improvement in Group B So certain intensity, times, exercise following discussion have meaning towards improve Physical self-perception and social physique anxiety in university girls
