AU  - Azim, D
TI  - Impact of Baby Spa (Hydrotherapy and Massage) on Motor Development and Regulation in Preterm Infants: A Randomized Controlled Trial
T2  - PHYSIKALISCHE MEDIZIN REHABILITATIONSMEDIZIN KURORTMEDIZIN
AB  - Objective This study aims to evaluate the effects of baby spa (hydrotherapy and massage) in addition to neurodevelopmental physiotherapy on motor development and regulation capacity in preterm infants. Material and Methods In this prospective, randomized controlled study, 24 preterm infants with a corrected age of 2-6 months and stable medical conditions were included (12 infants per group). The infants were divided into control (standard neurodevelopmental physiotherapy) and intervention (physiotherapy+baby spa) groups. The intervention group received 2 sessions of standard physiotherapy and 2 sessions of baby spa (floating+massage) per week. Evaluations included the Alberta Infant Motor Scale (AIMS), Hammersmith Infant Neurological Examination (HINE), Infant Sleep and Regulation Diary, and anthropometric measurements. Results Significant improvements in motor development, neurological maturation, and sleep duration were observed in both groups (p<0.05). AIMS total scores showed improvement in both groups, but effect size analysis indicated higher effects in the intervention group. The intervention group showed an average increase of 1.5 hours in night sleep duration, while the control group exhibited a smaller increase. No significant between-group differences were found (p>0.05). Conclusion Although baby spa interventions did not show statistical superiority over standard physiotherapy, they may serve as a safe, complementary approach to enhance quality of life in preterm infants. The improvements in motor development, neurological maturation, and sleep suggest that baby spa could be a valuable addition to early intervention programs. Further studies with larger sample sizes and long-term follow-up are needed to confirm its broader benefits. ruppe eine geringere Zunahme von 0,7 Stunden verzeichnete. Es wurden keine signifikanten Unterschiede zwischen den Gruppen festgestellt (p>0,05).
